{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778601787879,"sessionID":"ses_1e315425effe0RCZbeqO4dCgzX","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"grep","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-b89747a70e9d7a2f","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"pattern":"CD14"},"output":"Found 733 matches (showing first 100)\n/home/runner/work/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__issue_context__.json:\n  Line 2:   \"body\": \"**CL term**\\n[CL_0001057](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0001057) — myeloid dendritic cell, human\\n[CL_0001058](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0001058) — plasmacytoid dendritic cell, human\\n\\n**Suggested revision of class hierarchy**\\nAdd: lacks expression of CD14\\nAlthough there were previous attempts to annotate DCs with CD14-negative expression (Issue [#123](https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/123), [#125](https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/125), [#127](https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/127)), these were unfortunately closed without resolution.\\n\\n**textual def**\\nplease add in the textual def that this cell is CD14-negative.\\n\\n**References**  **Add these references but do not replace exisiting references**\\n\\nRelevant OMIP references demonstrating standard gating strategy:\\n1. [OMIP-77](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34260151/)\\n2. [OMIP-87](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36470845/)\\n3. [OMIP-94](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37254600/)\\n4. [OMIP-101](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38343094/)\\n5. [OMIP-112](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40095400/)\\n\\n\\nThese human dendritic cell populations are consistently defined as CD14-negative in flow cytometry gating strategies. Although some tissue-specific DC subsets (e.g., dermal DCs) may express CD14, the general population of human DCs is gated as CD14⁻ to distinguish them from monocytes.\\n\\n\\nThank you for your consideration.\\n\",\n\n  Line 25:       \"body\": \"@addiehl could you please check this out ? I agree with adding lack CD14 expression to \\\"[CL_0001057](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0001057) — myeloid dendritic cell, human and [CL_0001058](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0001058) — plasmacytoid dendritic cell, human\\\". However, I am not sure about adding it to the general human term [CL_0001056](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0001056) — dendritic cell, human as there are some human dendritic cells that do express CD14 (PMID: [23621371](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23621371/), [PMID: 34279540](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8292131/). Thank you \",\n\n  Line 35:   \"title\": \"[Class hierarchy] Add \\\"CD14 lacks\\\" to human dendritic cell terms\",\n\n\n/home/runner/work/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__pr_result__/src/patterns/definitions.owl:\n  Line 1050: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> \"DOI:10.1038/nri.2017.28\") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> \"DOI:10.1111/sji.12883\") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> \"DOI:10.1182/blood-2009-07-235028\") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> \"DOI:10.3389/fimmu.2015.00423/full\") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> \"DOI:10.3389/fimmu.2019.02035\") <http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000860> \"Classical monocytes are a subtype of monocytes that are characterized by high CD14 but no CD16 expression. Emerging from the bone marrow and entering the bloodstream, these cells play central roles in immune responses and regulation of inflammation. CD14-positive CD16-negative monocytes form the majority of circulating monocytes in the body, typically contributing to around 80-90% of the total monocyte pool. \n\n  Line 1068: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> \"DOI:10.1016/j.immuni.2018.10.005\") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> \"DOI:10.1111/sji.12883\") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> \"DOI:10.1146/annurev-immunol-042617-053119\") <http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000875> \"Non-classical monocytes, also known as patrolling monocytes, are a subtype of monocytes in the circulation, accounting for approximately 5-10% of total human monocytes. These monocytes are characterized by the differential expression of two surface markers, CD14 and CD16, with non-classical monocytes expressing CD14 and high levels of CD16 (CD14+ CD16++). Classical monocytes are released from the bone marrow into the circulation and can transition to intermediate followed by non-classical monocytes.\n\n  Line 1491: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> \"DOI:10.1182/blood-2010-02-258558\") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> \"DOI:10.1182/blood-2010-12-326355\") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> \"DOI:10.2147/JIR.S292513\") <http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0002393> \"Intermediate monocytes, also defined as CD14++CD16+ monocytes, constitute one of the three subsets of human monocytes which also include classical and non-classical monocytes. These cells play a crucial role in the physiological function of the immune system and are particularly significant in the human body's response to inflammation and infection. With a diverse array of functions, intermediate monocytes uniquely contribute to the immune response potency by bridging the gap between the classical and non-classical monocyte subsets.\n\n  Line 1492: Intermediate monocytes exhibit a combined functionality of the two other subsets in terms of their phenotypic characteristics, functional capabilities and gene expression profiles. They express high levels of CD14 (like classical monocytes) and also express CD16 (like non-classical monocytes). These monocytes generally release lower amounts of cytokines compared to classical and non-classical monocytes, but produce the most reactive oxygen species. Intermediate monocytes may also induce T cell proliferation and express high levels of major histocompatibility complex molecules for antigen presentation.\n\n\n/home/runner/work/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__pr_result__/src/patterns/data/default/ExtendedDescription.tsv:\n  Line 603: CL:0000860\tCL:0000860\tCL_0000860\t\"Classical monocytes are a subtype of monocytes that are characterized by high CD14 but no CD16 expression. Emerging from the bone marrow and entering the bloodstream, these cells play central roles in immune responses and regulation of inflammation. CD14-positive CD16-negative monocytes form the majority of circulating monocytes in the body, typically contributing to around 80-90% of the total monocyte pool. \n\n  Line 1620: CL:0000875\tCL:0000875\tCL_0000875\t\"Non-classical monocytes, also known as patrolling monocytes, are a subtype of monocytes in the circulation, accounting for approximately 5-10% of total human monocytes. These monocytes are characterized by the differential expression of two surface markers, CD14 and CD16, with non-classical monocytes expressing CD14 and high levels of CD16 (CD14+ CD16++). Classical monocytes are released from the bone marrow into the circulation and can transition to intermediate followed by non-classical monocytes.\n\n  Line 1677: CL:0002393\tCL:0002393\tCL_0002393\t\"Intermediate monocytes, also defined as CD14++CD16+ monocytes, constitute one of the three subsets of human monocytes which also include classical and non-classical monocytes. These cells play a crucial role in the physiological function of the immune system and are particularly significant in the human body's response to inflammation and infection. With a diverse array of functions, intermediate monocytes uniquely contribute to the immune response potency by bridging the gap between the classical and non-classical monocyte subsets.\n\n  Line 1679: Intermediate monocytes exhibit a combined functionality of the two other subsets in terms of their phenotypic characteristics, functional capabilities and gene expression profiles. They express high levels of CD14 (like classical monocytes) and also express CD16 (like non-classical monocytes). These monocytes generally release lower amounts of cytokines compared to classical and non-classical monocytes, but produce the most reactive oxygen species. Intermediate monocytes may also induce T cell proliferation and express high levels of major histocompatibility complex molecules for antigen presentation.\n\n\n/home/runner/work/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__pr_result__/src/ontology/reports/summary_release.md:\n  Line 254: | fibrochondrocyte progenitor cell (CL:0020021) | A mesenchymal progenitor cell located in fibrocartilaginous tissues, along the fibrochondrocytic differentiation pathway that co-expresses both fibrochondrocyte markers (COL1A1, COL3A1) and mesenchymal stem cell markers (MCAM/CD146, MYLK) in humans. This cell serves as a progenitor for mature fibrochondrocytes and other meniscal cell types, with differentiation regulated by TGF-β signaling, focal adhesion, and extracellular matrix-receptor interaction pathways. |\n\n\n/home/runner/work/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__pr_result__/src/ontology/reports/release-diff.md:\n  Line 2446: - [fibrochondrocyte progenitor cell](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0020021) [definition](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115) \"A mesenchymal progenitor cell located in fibrocartilaginous tissues, along the fibrochondrocytic differentiation pathway that co-expresses both fibrochondrocyte markers (COL1A1, COL3A1) and mesenchymal stem cell markers (MCAM/CD146, MYLK) in humans. This cell serves as a progenitor for mature fibrochondrocytes and other meniscal cell types, with differentiation regulated by TGF-&beta; signaling, focal adhesion, and extracellular matrix-receptor interaction pathways.\" \n\n\n/home/runner/work/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__pr_result__/src/ontology/reports/cl-base-diff.md:\n  Line 1146: - [fibrochondrocyte progenitor cell](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0020021) [definition](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115) \"A mesenchymal progenitor cell located in fibrocartilaginous tissues, along the fibrochondrocytic differentiation pathway that co-expresses both fibrochondrocyte markers (COL1A1, COL3A1) and mesenchymal stem cell markers (MCAM/CD146, MYLK) in humans. This cell serves as a progenitor for mature fibrochondrocytes and other meniscal cell types, with differentiation regulated by TGF-&beta; signaling, focal adhesion, and extracellular matrix-receptor interaction pathways.\"^^[string](http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string) \n\n\n/home/runner/work/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__pr_result__/src/ontology/imports/merged_import.owl:\n  Line 147303: AnnotationAssertion(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PR_000001444> \"CD144\")\n\n  Line 148064: # Class: <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PR_000001889> (CD14 molecule)\n\n  Line 148066: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> \"PRO:WCB\") <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PR_000001889> \"A protein that is a translation product of the human CD14 gene or a 1:1 ortholog thereof.\")\n\n  Line 148068: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> \"PRO:DNx\") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasSynonymType> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/pr#PRO-short-label>) <http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PR_000001889> \"CD14\")\n\n  Line 148069: AnnotationAssertion(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym> <http://purl.obol